      {
         "id": "SECTION_introduction",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "### Introduction\n\nIn the previous texts, we studied how humans measured the size of the Earth (<htcite0>) and how different peoples drew maps with a specific region at the center (<htcite1>). However, the center of the Earth is not on its surface: it is inside the planet.\n\nIn this text, we will continue studying inside our planet.",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "7c7a3259-88e0-47b8-920c-9a5baacdb212",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "The Size of the Earth",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "",
                        "year": "",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "22080ce2-a428-493e-84f9-aebdbed90b0c",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "I live on the planet...",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2026",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection question_mark\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-brain\"></i></div><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\"><b>Reflection 1: What is inside the Earth?</b><br /><br />Beyond what we can see, what do you think Earth has inside it?</div></div></center></p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
      },
      {
         "id": "SECTION_local_content",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "<h3>Earth: A \"Living\" Planet</h3>When discussing maps, it is essential to consider geological time, as continents have not always existed in their present form. Besides the continental landmasses, we must also account for tectonic plate movements, which play a major role in the formation and transformation of visible land structures (Figure 1)",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "021534bf-9945-43c6-ae30-7d1bcd43aecd",
                     "text": "Windley, Brian Frederick",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2024",
                        "month": "04",
                        "day": "05"
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "2440434d-29ba-4475-814e-a15eec9e63c2",
                     "text": "Andel, Tjeerd H. van and Murphy, J. Brendan",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2024",
                        "month": "09",
                        "day": "10"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": "."
            },
            {
               "text": "What exactly are tectonic plates? The hard outer layer of the Earth — the lithosphere — is made of the crust we live on and the rigid uppermost part of the mantle beneath it. It is not one single continuous piece. It is broken into large slabs of hard rock called tectonic plates, which are pieces of the lithosphere that move slowly over the hotter, softer rock beneath them. The plates are always moving, but very slowly: a few centimeters per year, about as fast as your fingernails grow. We hardly notice this movement, but where plates meet they pull apart, push into each other, or scrape side by side — and that is where most earthquakes, volcanoes, and mountain ranges come from",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "2440434d-29ba-4475-814e-a15eec9e63c2",
                     "text": "Andel, Tjeerd H. van and Murphy, J. Brendan",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2024",
                        "month": "09",
                        "day": "10"
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "021534bf-9945-43c6-ae30-7d1bcd43aecd",
                     "text": "Windley, Brian Frederick",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2024",
                        "month": "04",
                        "day": "05"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": "."
            },
            {
               "text": "Figure 1 shows the main plates and the regions of the world they cover (<htcite0>):\n\n- **Eurasian plate**: Europe and most of Asia\n- **North American plate**: North America, Greenland, and the western North Atlantic Ocean\n- **South American plate**: South America and the western South Atlantic Ocean\n- **African plate**: Africa and the ocean floor around it\n- **Antarctic plate**: Antarctica and the surrounding seas\n- **Australian plate**: Australia and the surrounding ocean floor\n- **Indian plate**: India and the Indian Ocean to its south\n- **Pacific plate**: almost the entire Pacific Ocean\n- **Nazca plate**: the ocean floor west of South America\n\nSmaller plates fill the spaces in between, such as the **Caribbean**, **Arabian**, **Philippine**, **Cocos**, **Juan de Fuca**, and **Scotia** plates",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "196ca955-cd62-4548-a527-ada3858d16ec",
                     "text": "Tectonic Plates of Earth",
                     "page": "Earthquake Science Center",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2026",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p class=\"desc\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"htImageZoomButton\" onclick=\"htImageZoom('imgTectonicPlates', '0%')\"><img id=\"imgTectonicPlates\" src=\"\" class=\"imgcenter\" alt=\"World map showing the tectonic plates as colored regions, with labels such as Eurasian, North American, Pacific, Nazca, South American, African, and Australian plates, and the Equator marked.\"/></button><b>Figure 1</b>: The tectonic plates divide the Earth's crust into distinct \"plates\" that are always slowly moving. Earthquakes are concentrated along these plate boundaries. © Earthquake Science Center (<htcite0>).</p>",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "196ca955-cd62-4548-a527-ada3858d16ec",
                     "text": "Tectonic Plates of Earth",
                     "page": "Earthquake Science Center",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2026",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<h4>In the past</h4>Continents have not always sat where modern maps show them. If we could travel far back in time, we would see the landmasses gathered together and pulled apart again and again. Figure 2 takes us on such a journey: it shows the breakup of the supercontinent Pangea in three stages — about 237 million years ago, 152 million years ago, and 66 million years ago. Before visiting each of these moments, let us see how such a huge supercontinent formed and began to break apart",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "8634e87f-9ea9-4fdd-9a26-ae3b33e17af1",
                     "text": "Britannica, T. Editors of Encyclopaedia",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2024",
                        "month": "07",
                        "day": "23"
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "0d5310dc-12ba-4911-b451-ea98277fcffc",
                     "text": "Breakup of Pangea",
                     "page": "U.S. Geological Survey",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2026",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": "."
            },
            {
               "text": "Before continents settled into their current configuration, Earth went through periods dominated by supercontinents. One example is Gondwana, a massive landmass that included what are now South America (<i>Abya Yala</i>), Africa, India, Australia, and Antarctica. This supercontinent encompassed the region from which our species would later emerge: Africa",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "59c0b962-d032-4b6c-9498-2341e089510b",
                     "text": "Allmon, D. W. et al",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "750e84b5-9330-4f99-92b0-72469a6a64f4",
                     "text": "Allmon, D. W. et al",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "d8dee061-0e72-4ca5-bfe3-bcfa3c42b070",
                     "text": "Redacción National Geographic",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2024",
                        "month": "07",
                        "day": "08"
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "9b86417a-a5bf-4211-bc42-ee8a1d3739c0",
                     "text": "Britannica, T. Editors of Encyclopaedia",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2024",
                        "month": "10",
                        "day": "14"
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "06780f2e-073c-4da7-869e-945ecd275316",
                     "text": "Stampfli, G. M., et al.",
                     "page": " pp. 2 - 10",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2013",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": "."
            },
            {
               "text": "Over millions of years, Gondwana joined with other landmasses to form the supercontinent Pangea. Eventually, Pangea began to break apart due to tectonic plate movement, a process that, according to the most widely accepted geological models, led to the formation of the continents as we know them today (Figure 2)",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "8634e87f-9ea9-4fdd-9a26-ae3b33e17af1",
                     "text": "Britannica, T. Editors of Encyclopaedia",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2024",
                        "month": "07",
                        "day": "23"
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "06780f2e-073c-4da7-869e-945ecd275316",
                     "text": "Stampfli, G. M.",
                     "page": "et al.",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2013",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": "."
            },
            {
               "text": "Our first stop is about 237 million years ago, in the Middle Triassic — the top globe of Figure 2. Almost all of the continents were locked together in one giant landmass called Pangea, a name that means \"all land\". North America lay right beside Africa, South America was pressed against Africa, and Europe was joined to Asia. The southern half of Pangea was Gondwana, and a vast ocean called Panthalassa surrounded the supercontinent. There was no Atlantic Ocean yet: the lands we know today were still part of a single block",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "0d5310dc-12ba-4911-b451-ea98277fcffc",
                     "text": "Breakup of Pangea",
                     "page": "U.S. Geological Survey",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2026",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "06780f2e-073c-4da7-869e-945ecd275316",
                     "text": "Stampfli, G. M.",
                     "page": "et al.",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2013",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": "."
            },
            {
               "text": "Our next stop is about 152 million years ago, in the Late Jurassic — the middle globe of Figure 2. By then, Pangea had started to break apart. A narrow sea called the Proto-Atlantic Ocean was opening between North America and Africa, and the supercontinent was separating into two great pieces: Laurasia in the north and Gondwana in the south. As the plates continued to move, this young sea grew wider and wider",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "0d5310dc-12ba-4911-b451-ea98277fcffc",
                     "text": "Breakup of Pangea",
                     "page": "U.S. Geological Survey",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2026",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "06780f2e-073c-4da7-869e-945ecd275316",
                     "text": "Stampfli, G. M.",
                     "page": "et al.",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2013",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": "."
            },
            {
               "text": "Our last stop is 66 million years ago, at the K/T boundary — the bottom globe of Figure 2. The continents were already close to where they are today. The Atlantic Ocean had widened into a real ocean, separating the Americas from Europe and Africa, and the South Atlantic was opening between South America and Africa. India was moving quickly north toward Asia, while Australia and Antarctica were still close together. On this globe you can also see the site where an asteroid struck the Earth near Chicxulub, in today's Mexico, in the catastrophe that ended the age of dinosaurs. Only tens of millions of years separate this world from ours",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "0d5310dc-12ba-4911-b451-ea98277fcffc",
                     "text": "Breakup of Pangea",
                     "page": "U.S. Geological Survey",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2026",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "06780f2e-073c-4da7-869e-945ecd275316",
                     "text": "Stampfli, G. M.",
                     "page": "et al.",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2013",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": "."
            },
            {
               "text": "<p class=\"desc\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"htImageZoomButton\" onclick=\"htImageZoom('imgUSGSPangea', '0%')\"><img id=\"imgUSGSPangea\" src=\"\" class=\"imgcenter\" alt=\"The image has three subimages showing the breakup of Pangea: Middle Triassic (237 Ma), Late Jurassic (152 Ma), and K/T Boundary (66 Ma).\"/></button><b>Figure 2</b>: Breakup of Pangea, in three subimages: Middle Triassic (237 Ma); Late Jurassic (152 Ma); K/T Boundary (66 Ma). © U.S. Geological Survey (<htcite0>).</p>",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "0d5310dc-12ba-4911-b451-ea98277fcffc",
                     "text": "Breakup of Pangea",
                     "page": "U.S. Geological Survey",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2026",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
      },
      {
         "id": "SECTION_conclusion",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "### Conclusion\n\nIn this text, we saw that the continents we see on maps today did not exist millions of years ago. At several times in Earth's history, continents joined into larger landmasses such as Gondwana and Pangea, and the slow movement of the tectonic plates has been splitting them apart and bringing them together again ever since.\n\nThis is exactly what makes Earth a \"living\" planet: it is not static. Beneath our feet, the plates keep moving a few centimeters every year — about as fast as your fingernails grow. Millions of years from now, the map of the world will look different once again",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "8634e87f-9ea9-4fdd-9a26-ae3b33e17af1",
                     "text": "Britannica, T. Editors of Encyclopaedia",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2024",
                        "month": "07",
                        "day": "23"
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "06780f2e-073c-4da7-869e-945ecd275316",
                     "text": "Stampfli, G. M.",
                     "page": "et al.",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2013",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": "."
            }
         ]
      }
